  "item_one_liners": {},
  "label": "Stochastic Gene Expression Dynamics",
  "related": [
    "mechanisms-viral-a1e5",
    "self-growing-09be",
    "model-coupling-c52b"
  ],
  "secondary_categories": [],
  "slug": "age-framework-b5f8",
  "summary": "Exact stochastic simulation and cell-cycle-aware noise models now connect expression dynamics to growth, division and phenotypic heterogeneity.",
  "synthesis": "The four items share a focus on how stochastic molecular dynamics map onto cell-level growth and state. A review frames gene expression noise in growing and dividing cells, emphasizing cell-cycle fluctuation signatures, cyclostationary behavior and model fitting. Two items address mechanism directly: GeneRegulatorySystems.jl performs exact stochastic simulation of nonlinear gene regulation via a biophysical chemical master equation, and a stochastic model of Rtc-mediated RNA repair in E. coli reports bimodal translational capacity and switching between resistant and susceptible states. A thermodynamic model links transcriptome RNA folding energy shifts to growth state across the cell cycle, drug-stressed cancer cells and E. coli. The dominant thread is therefore the use of stochastic or thermodynamic descriptions to connect fluctuating expression to growth and phenotypic heterogeneity. What is new is the attribution of state switching and growth-state dependence to these dynamics. All four are preprints without published validation.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-07"
}
